WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.115
Least privilege for file access means the application process should have
only the file and directory permissions needed for its intended work.

2
00:00:08.115 --> 00:00:17.759
If a Python service only needs to write uploads into one storage area, it
should not have broad write access to application code, secrets, backups,
or unrelated tenant data.

3
00:00:17.759 --> 00:00:23.812
Permissions become especially important when several services, workers, or
users share infrastructure.

4
00:00:23.812 --> 00:00:26.139
Storage location is a security decision.

5
00:00:26.139 --> 00:00:38.910
Private storage, public storage, local filesystem paths, cloud object
storage at a high level, container volumes, mounted directories, backup
paths, and temporary work areas all have different exposure and lifecycle
rules.

6
00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:42.102
Publicly reachable paths should not be used as a default.

7
00:00:42.102 --> 00:00:46.958
Sensitive files should be stored where access can be authorized, logged,
and revoked.

8
00:00:46.958 --> 00:00:52.146
Uploads should be separated from application code and from secrets or
configuration files.

9
00:00:52.146 --> 00:00:57.600
Tenant or user data should be separated logically, and sometimes
physically, depending on risk.

10
00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:02.788
A mistake in one part of the system should not automatically expose every
other user's files.

11
00:01:02.788 --> 00:01:09.639
Storage design should reduce blast radius if a file is mishandled, a
worker is compromised, or a permissions rule is wrong.

12
00:01:09.639 --> 00:01:13.297
Backups and replicas need the same attention as primary storage.

13
00:01:13.297 --> 00:01:19.882
A file removed from the application may still exist in backups, object
versions, logs, exports, or derived previews.

14
00:01:19.882 --> 00:01:25.003
Secrets files and configuration files should have stricter access than
ordinary content.

15
00:01:25.003 --> 00:01:32.120
Strong storage design gives teams a clear answer to who can read, write,
list, delete, restore, and audit each category of file.
